WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.550
Collars, cuffs and waistbands that sit crisp and flat.

2
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:08.490
This is the video for module two of Fashion Finishing and Quality Control.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:16.200
Collars, cuffs and waistbands sit close to the face, hands and waist, so clients notice every bump.

4
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:24.980
The crisp finish comes from four skills: interfacing, trimming and grading seams, understitching, and careful matching.

5
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:41.100
Choose interfacing a little lighter than the fabric, and test-fuse a scrap first. Press, do not slide, often 10 to 15 seconds in each position. Keep the iron on its heel between presses, and let the collar cool flat.

6
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:56.200
Fabric loses a little length on the outside of a fold. So trim 2 to 3 mm off the outer edge of the under collar. The upper collar bubbles slightly, and that extra lets the seam roll underneath, where nobody sees it.

7
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:10.270
After sewing, grade the seam: under collar allowance to about 3 mm, upper to about 6 mm. Notch the curves. Then understitch the allowances to the under collar, turn, roll and press.

8
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.140
Sew every collar on a card template, so both ends match.

9
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:25.630
Stand collars must stand up evenly. Staystitch the neckline as soon as it is cut. A stretched neckline is the most common reason a stand wrinkles at the back.

10
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:32.170
A stand needs firmer interfacing than a flat collar, because it must hold itself upright.

11
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:43.760
For sharp shirt collar points, take one or two stitches across the point instead of pivoting. Trim, then push the point out with a point turner, never with sharp scissors.

12
00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:56.580
For example, Funmi in Port Harcourt is sewing a senator top. The stand is 42 cm between the centre front marks. But the sewn neckline measures 43.2 cm.

13
00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:13.400
It is 1.2 cm too long, so it stretched in handling. She runs a line of long stitches inside the seam allowance and pulls it up to 42 cm, mostly over the back and shoulders. Then she attaches the stand, with no ripples.

14
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:25.400
For example, Esther in Jos has a client with a 16 cm wrist. She adds 4 cm ease and a 2 cm overlap, for a finished cuff of 22 cm.

15
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:36.510
With 1 cm seam allowances, she cuts it 24 cm long. The cuff folds in half, so for a 6 cm cuff she cuts 14 cm high.

16
00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:49.090
The sleeve hem is 32 cm, but the cuff closes over 20 cm, so she gathers in 12 cm. She puts most of the fullness towards the back, where the elbow bends.

17
00:02:50.340 --> 00:03:05.380
Lay the two cuffs side by side. Check the same width, the same height, and buttonholes in the same position. Check the plackets lie flat and the gathers are even. Then close the cuff around a wrist. One or two fingers should fit inside.

18
00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:21.730
For example, Grace in Benin City has a client with a 76 cm waist. Waistband ease is small, so she adds 1.5 cm, plus a 3 cm underlap for the hook. The finished band is 80.5 cm.

19
00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:34.140
With 1.5 cm seam allowances, she cuts 83.5 cm long. For a 3.5 cm band that folds in half, she cuts 10 cm wide.

20
00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:45.220
She checks the skirt waist measures 77.5 cm after the darts, so it fits without stretching. Then she matches the marked band to the skirt.

21
00:03:46.470 --> 00:04:05.080
For elastic, a common start is 85 to 90 percent of the waist, but always test it on the body. Make the casing only 0.5 to 1 cm wider than the elastic, overlap the ends 2 cm, and stitch in the ditch at the side seams so it cannot twist or roll.

22
00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:15.030
A faced waist has no band. Interface the facing, trim, clip and understitch it, then tack it to the darts, seams and zip tape.

23
00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:32.410
To remember. Trim the under collar slightly smaller. Staystitch and measure necklines before attaching. Calculate cuffs and waistbands from the body, plus ease, overlap and allowances. Check cuffs as a pair. And understitch every facing.

24
00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:44.450
Now sew a sample Peter Pan collar and a mandarin stand, calculate a cuff and sew a bound placket, and calculate a waistband with a short elastic casing sample.

25
00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.740
Continue with the lessons below.
